---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Interaction">
  <p>
    ArrowCrab lets you navigate graphs with pan and zoom, open up compound nodes
    with expand/collapse, and trace dependencies with causal cone highlighting.
  </p>

  <h2>Pan and Zoom</h2>
  <p>
    All viewport interaction is handled by <code>viewport.ts</code>, which
    attaches a single <code>AbortController</code>-managed set of event
    listeners to the canvas and window. Cleanup is a single
    <code>abort()</code> call.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Input</th><th>Action</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Click-drag</td><td>Pan</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scroll wheel</td><td>Zoom centered on cursor</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>+</code> / <code>=</code></td><td>Zoom in (canvas center)</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>Zoom out (canvas center)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Fit graph to screen</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Pan by 50 px</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pinch (touch)</td><td>Zoom centered on pinch midpoint</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Single-finger drag</td><td>Pan</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Zoom range is 10% to 500%. Zoom is cursor-centered: the point under your
    cursor stays put while the graph scales around it.
  </p>

  <h3>Click vs. Drag</h3>
  <p>
    Less than 5 pixels of movement between mousedown and mouseup counts as a
    click. More than that is a drag. This prevents accidental selections when
    you meant to pan.
  </p>

  <h2>Expand and Collapse</h2>
  <p>
    Compound nodes (nodes containing subgraphs) display a
    <code>[+]</code>/<code>[&minus;]</code> toggle in their top-left corner. Clicking
    it triggers <code>toggleNodeExpansion</code>, which:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Adds or removes the node from the expanded-node set.</li>
    <li>Computes child scope layouts if expanding for the first time.</li>
    <li>
      Recomputes the layout chain upward to the top-level scope (since the
      parent node's size changes).
    </li>
    <li>Recomputes all edge routing.</li>
    <li>Re-renders.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Hit regions are recorded during each render pass for visible nodes only.
    Since they're rebuilt every frame, they're always in sync with the current
    viewport and layout.
  </p>

  <h2>Selection</h2>
  <p>
    Clicking a node or edge selects it. <code>Ctrl+click</code>
    (or <code>Cmd+click</code> on macOS) adds to the selection without clearing it.
    Clicking empty space clears all selections.
  </p>

  <h3>Hit Testing Priority</h3>
  <p>When you click, hit regions are checked in this order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Toggle buttons</strong> - highest priority, triggers expand/collapse.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Leaf nodes</strong> - rectangular hit test.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Edges</strong> - polyline proximity check within 8 px.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expanded nodes</strong> - checked last so edges and child nodes
      inside them stay clickable.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Empty space</strong> - clears selection.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Expanded nodes are checked after edges on purpose. Clicking an edge inside
    an expanded node selects the edge, not the container. The inside of an
    expanded node is basically transparent to clicks until nothing else matches.
  </p>

  <h2>Causal Cone Highlighting</h2>
  <p>
    When you select nodes, ArrowCrab highlights their
    <strong>causal cone</strong>: all ancestors and descendants reachable by
    following edges in both directions. Everything outside the cone dims to 15%
    opacity.
  </p>
  <p>
    It runs BFS in both directions from the selected nodes over a global
    adjacency map. Edges where both endpoints are reachable get highlighted too.
    The adjacency map is cached and only rebuilt when the expansion state
    changes, so repeated clicks are fast even on big graphs.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lets you answer "what depends on X?" and "what does X depend on?" in
    one click. Multi-selection (Ctrl+click) shows the union of all selected
    nodes' cones.
  </p>
  <p>
    Selecting an edge highlights it and both endpoints, merged into the causal
    cone if nodes are also selected.
  </p>

  <h2>File Loading</h2>
  <p>You can load JSON files two ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Drag and drop</strong> a <code>.json</code> file onto the landing zone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Click to browse</strong> and pick a file.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On load, it deserializes the JSON, builds the graph model, runs layout and
    routing, and centers the viewport. The "Load JSON" button in the top-right
    goes back to the file picker.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
